Linked List Group Reverse Difficulty: HardAccuracy: 57.08%Submissions: 260K+Points: 8Average Time: 30m You are given the head of a Singly linked list. You have to reverse every k node in the linked list and return the head of the modified list. Note: If the number of nodes is not a multiple of k then the left-out nodes at the end, should be considered as a group and must be reversed. Examples: Input: k = 2, Output: 2 -> 1 -> 4 -> 3 -> 6 -> 5



---------code-----------------------
class Solution {
  public:
    Node* reverseKGroup(Node* head, int k) {
        if (!head) return nullptr;

        Node* curr = head;
        Node* prev = nullptr;
        Node* next = nullptr;
        int count = 0;

        // Check if there are at least k nodes to reverse
        Node* temp = head;
        int nodeCount = 0;
        while (temp && nodeCount < k) {
            temp = temp->next;
            nodeCount++;
        }
        if (nodeCount < k) {
            // Reverse the remaining nodes as well
            k = nodeCount;
        }

        count = 0;
        // Reverse k nodes
        while (curr && count < k) {
            next = curr->next;
            curr->next = prev;
            prev = curr;
            curr = next;
            count++;
        }

        // Recursively call for the next part of the list
        if (next != nullptr) {
            head->next = reverseKGroup(next, k);
        }

        // prev is the new head after reversing
        return prev;
    }
};


--------------------------------------------

✅ How it works:

Count k nodes from current position. If nodes are less than k, reverse whatever is left.

Reverse the first k nodes iteratively.

Recursively reverse the rest of the list and connect to the current group.

Return the new head of the reversed group.

Example:

Input: 1->2->3->4->5->6 and k = 2
Output: 2->1->4->3->6->5

This handles all cases, including when the last group has fewer than k nodes.
